  "honest_finding": "Per-grade accuracy does NOT collapse the way MASTER_PLAN.md's a-priori expectation predicted (\"fails on grades 1, 3, and 4\"). What the data actually show: grade(s) [1] genuinely collapse toward chance ([45.8]% accuracy), while grade(s) [0, 2, 3, 4] are all reasonably well predicted ([94.3, 72.6, 74.5, 72.1]% accuracy) -- grades 3 and 4 (Severe/Proliferative) are NOT among the weak ones, contrary to the pre-registered expectation. Only grade 1 (Mild NPDR, the subtlest/hardest-to-grade category) truly collapses. Separately: the AGGREGATE lookup-table QWK tracks the raw inter-eye QWK almost exactly at every stratification level (see Figure 2 right panel) -- the shortcut is not weaker in aggregate, it is weaker specifically for one grade. Both correlation and lookup QWK far exceed the permuted-patient null at every stratification level. Report the ACTUAL per-grade pattern (grade 1 only) in the paper, not the plan's predicted one -- this is a stronger, more precise finding than the a-priori guess."
